{{- define "submission-phase" -}}

--- SUBMISSION (MANDATORY - DO NOT SKIP) ---
After implementation and tests pass, you MUST submit for review:
{{- if .AcceptanceSource}}
Commit the declared acceptance manifest with a complete obligation-to-proof mapping first.
Submission reruns canonical validation on clean committed code after rebase. Missing mappings,
failed commands, dirty files, or stale source/commit evidence block before reviewer assignment.
Before submitting, run focused tests, not the full canonical list. After an admission failure,
re-run only the failing command, unless it uses a single_use runtime input: only the gate runs
that one, so fix and resubmit.
Submission may queue for admission slots before its batch budget starts, so it can run far past
timeout_seconds; its stderr reports what it waits for. Never interrupt it or start another submit for this task.
{{- end}}
1. Stage the intended files for this task: git -C {{.Worktree}} add <files>
2. Create the submission commit for this task using the required git commit workflow for this environment.
3. Verify the worktree is clean: git -C {{.Worktree}} status --short
4. Capture git -C {{.Worktree}} rev-parse HEAD once as <submission-sha>; reuse the full SHA on retry.
5. Execute: {{binaryName}} submit-for-review {{.TaskID}} <submission-sha> --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
6. Wait for completion and follow result.safe_action with the returned result.task_status (see LIFECYCLE RESULTS).
7. Only after COMPLETED or ALREADY_COMPLETED with safe_action=continue, run {{binaryName}} await-verdict (see TOOLS for loop semantics).
   On REJECTED: task is already reclaimed — read the rejection reason, fix, and resubmit.

❌ FAILURE MODE: Completing work, saying "ready for review", then exiting WITHOUT running the command.
Submission needs a confirmed lifecycle result; a stopped or uncertain call is not proof that this request submitted.
{{- end -}}
